WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.110
Stop. Before your team builds a RAG pipeline,

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:04.490
please watch this video.

3
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.060
RAG is five tools.

4
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.540
It's a loader, a splitter, an embedder, a vector database,

5
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.980
and then a re-ranker.

6
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.430
You assemble them and then you have to maintain each and every one.

7
00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.060
KnowDrive is one thing.

8
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.050
It's a store you read from and you write to.

9
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.710
Here are the nine differences that make KnowDrive different.

10
00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.080
One, there's zero pipeline.

11
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.560
You can just upload files and you can search them.

12
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.490
Video, audio, images, text files, documents, URLs,

13
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.470
they're all just searchable.

14
00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:39.500
There's no re-ranker, no chunker, no embedder.

15
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.120
There's just KnowDrive.

16
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.940
It's all handled for you.

17
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.250
You don't have to build it and you don't have to babysit it.

18
00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.240
Two, files stay files in KnowDrive.

19
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.560
A typical vector database kind of chunks file pieces

20
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.740
and leaves orphaned chunks.

21
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.300
We call a piece of a file an atom.

22
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.690
Every atom stays connected to its source file, path, and version.

23
00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.510
You can read the original, list its atoms,

24
00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.590
re-tag or revert the file.

25
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:12.740
Three, context cost stays flat across any corpus size.

26
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.330
A search hands back a ranked window that you can page through

27
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.760
and the files and bytes stay outside of the context window.

28
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.390
That same call pattern works whether there's 17,000 files,

29
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.540
170,000 files, or 1.7 million files.

30
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.740
It doesn't matter.

31
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.000
Four, multimodal in one store.

32
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:41.510
Text, image, video, audio is all on the same atoms search layer.

33
00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:45.690
So no pipeline for individual modalities.

34
00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.860
Five, governance is built in.

35
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.710
Tags, access control for each store,

36
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:55.020
scoped tokens that you can mint yourself.

37
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.270
Most RAG setups tack this on at the end

38
00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.360
as an additional layer to manage.

39
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.660
Six, it's made for agents.

40
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.180
MCP first, REST for large files.

41
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:10.540
It describes its own capabilities and skills to the agents

42
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.880
and it can use a corpus with no custom retrieval code.

43
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.340
Seven, you can change your mind.

44
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.440
You can update a file, re-tag it, delete it, revert it in place

45
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.820
without having a whole new build.

46
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.340
Most RAG indexes are write-once in practice.

47
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.940
Eight, this is a big one.

48
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.450
Corpus-wide queries on any model.

49
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.980
Normal RAG makes you pick a narrow top-k that misses the breadth

50
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.890
or a stuffed context window that costs more and caps out.

51
00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:48.700
KnowDrive's atoms layer draws only the atoms it needs for a response.

52
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.080
So one question can span the entirety of the corpus

53
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.130
and still fit into a tiny context window.

54
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.430
Corpus size is finally decoupled from context window size.

55
00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.650
So the same store that can serve a thousand tiny Qwen models

56
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.290
can also serve very large frontier models.

57
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.180
Nine, it's multiplayer.

58
00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.440
A normal RAG pipeline is built for one project

59
00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.460
and tuned for one model.

60
00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.460
A KnowDrive store is shared.

61
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.650
So every agent, every team is reading and writing to the same store,

62
00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:26.030
whether it's Codex, Claude Code or Cursor

63
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.860
or your own vibe-coded IDE.

64
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.860
They can all access the exact same store of knowledge.

65
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.250
So that's the difference.

66
00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.990
Normal RAG is a retrieval pipeline

67
00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.470
that you have to cobble together and then maintain.

68
00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:42.190
KnowDrive is a managed knowledge store

69
00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.020
with enterprise-grade entitlements

70
00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.487
that you just read and write to.

71
00:03:47.507 --> 00:03:49.730
Start free at knowdrive.ai.

72
00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.500
Get your 500 megs for free.

73
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.250
Use it with a couple of friends and put in a hundred files

74
00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:55.600
and just play around.
